    calloutWrap.innerHTML = "<div style=\"background:#1a1a1a;color:#ffffff;border-radius:6px;padding:20px 24px;line-height:1.7;font-size:0.95rem;\">"
      + "<strong style=\"display:block;margin-bottom:10px;font-size:1rem;color:#ffffff;\">Key Findings: " + data.month + "</strong>"
      + "<p style=\"margin:0 0 14px;color:#c8c8c8;font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.6;\">Seven retailers. 259,656 listings. E VS1 Round Excellent IGI.</p>"
      + "<ul style=\"margin:0;padding:0 0 0 18px;color:#ffffff;line-height:1.65;\">"
      + "<li style=\"margin:0 0 9px;\"><strong>$223 to $856 for the same 1ct stone, depending on where you buy.</strong> That is the published price range for a 1ct E VS1 IGI round this month, from four of the six IGI retailers (Ritani, Grown Brilliance, With Clarity, Brilliant Earth); Clean Origin and Blue Nile have no published 1ct figure this edition (see the Coverage notes below). At 2ct the published range runs from $496 to $2,730 across five retailers.</li>"
      + "<li style=\"margin:0 0 9px;\"><strong>Ritani cut prices, reversing July's increase, with the panel's largest moves at 1ct and 1.5ct.</strong> Its 1ct median fell from $295 to $223 per carat (-24%), 1.5ct from $258 to $212 (-18%), and 2ct from $266 to $248 (-7%). Part of the 1ct figure is a coverage change, not a price move: this edition's collection covers the full 0.95-1.05ct cell (July's band began at 1.00ct), and on July's narrower 1.00-1.05ct window Ritani's August median is $245 per carat (n=296), a like-for-like -17%. The 1ct sample is 1,126 stones this month against 302 in July.</li>"
      + "<li style=\"margin:0 0 9px;\"><strong>Brilliant Earth moved, and downward, after two editions unchanged at 1.5ct and 2ct.</strong> Having held $1,212 per carat at 1.5ct and $1,642 at 2ct in both June and July, it posted -5% at 1ct ($899 to $856), -5% at 1.5ct ($1,212 to $1,151), and -17% at 2ct ($1,642 to $1,365). It remains the IGI panel's premium end in every published cell.</li>"
      + "<li style=\"margin:0 0 9px;\"><strong>Grown Brilliance is back at 1ct: $500 per carat (n=795).</strong> Its band collection returned 61,542 listings this month against 4,141 in July, nearly fifteen times as many, when the 1ct cell held a single stone and went unpublished. July's reading of that cell as genuine inventory scarcity did not hold: the same collection method, one month later, returned the inventory. July's single 1ct stone was itself listed at $500 per carat, identical to this month's median, and the 1.5ct ($648) and 2ct ($659) medians are within $2 of July's, so July's small sample was not price-biased. Against June's last published 1ct figure ($576), Grown Brilliance is -13% over two months. Note that Grown Brilliance's 1ct figure covers 1.00-1.05ct: its collection returned no listings between 0.95 and 0.99ct in either month.</li>"
      + "<li style=\"margin:0 0 9px;\"><strong>Clean Origin's listed inventory contracted sharply, and only its 2ct cell is published: $278 per carat, -34% from July's $421.</strong> Clean Origin's own site count for round stones between 0.90ct and 2.50ct was 5,247 at collection (the delivered file holds 5,103 round listings), against 17,764 round listings delivered in July's collection and 3,982 in June's; its E-color round inventory in the 1ct cell fell from 2,546 listings to 51 (one of them VS1), so the 1ct and 1.5ct cells (n=1 and n=24) are not published. This closes July's open question about Clean Origin's June-to-July inventory jump only partially: the listed count swings widely month to month, and this index reports the count without knowing the cause.</li>"
      + "<li style=\"margin:0 0 9px;\"><strong>Blue Nile has no published IGI cell this month, so a labeled GIA reference row is shown; VRAI repriced its inventory upward.</strong> Blue Nile's E VS1 IGI cells hold 18, 5 and 27 listings on a full-inventory pull of similar size to July's (28,836 listings against 29,857). Its 1ct cell was equally thin in July (n=18): genuinely thin E VS1 IGI inventory. Its 1.5ct and 2ct cells (47 and 54 in July) thinned because a larger share of its E-color round inventory now carries GCAL or GIA reports rather than IGI, and VS1 remains a minority clarity grade; a share shift, not a collection issue. Because Blue Nile is otherwise absent from the table, its GIA-graded E VS1 Excellent stones are shown as a separate reference row, the same treatment VRAI gets: $988 per carat at 2ct (n=191), no publishable GIA figure at 1ct (n=0) or 1.5ct (n=3). That row is not part of the Market Median or the IGI price ranges, and it is not comparable to the IGI rows: GIA and IGI reports are different grading regimes, and in this month's data GIA-graded stones list above IGI-graded ones at every panel retailer where both appear in a cell; Blue Nile itself is the caveat to that pattern: its GIA and IGI 2ct E VS1 medians tracked within $1 per carat of each other from April through July (identical in April and July, $1 apart in May and June) before separating by $165 in August, $988 GIA on 191 stones against $823 for the 27 IGI stones, a sample too small to publish and too small to say whether that separation is real. VRAI, tracked as a non-IGI reference point, raised prices across its inventory: the 413 lots listed in both months rose a median 76% (on VRAI's own cell windows: +33% for the 18 lots in the 1ct window, 1.0-1.09ct D-F, and +69% for the 62 lots in the 1.5ct window, 1.6-1.9ct D-G). Its 1ct cell is published at $1,583 per carat (n=36), +34% against July's unpublished $1,180 (n=29) and +37% against June's last published $1,159; its 1.5ct cell's median stone price is $2,160 (1.6-1.9ct window, median 1.75ct) against $1,229 in July. This index verified the change against VRAI's own listings; it is VRAI's pricing, not a currency or collection effect.</li>"
      + "<li style=\"margin:0;\"><strong>With Clarity is essentially flat, and only the 1.5ct Market Median is published.</strong> With Clarity's collection was blocked at the first request from the home connection and completed the same evening from three successive mobile-network IPs, each blocked in turn after roughly 200 requests: $628 per carat at 1ct (n=536, full capture, +$8 versus July), $691 at 1.5ct (n=910, a partial capture of 85% of the band collected in price-ascending order, so a floor on the true median; July's $698 was a 74% capture of the same kind), and $694 at 2ct (n=694, full capture, -$34). A Market Median is published this month only where every retailer in the cell also had a published figure in July and the blended move agrees with the same-retailers move to within five percentage points. The 1.5ct Market Median passes: $1,005 (Ritani, Grown Brilliance, With Clarity, Brilliant Earth) against July's six-retailer $1,011, -0.6% blended and -0.6% on the same four retailers. The 1ct cell fails the first test because Grown Brilliance was not published in July (a blended $564 against July's unpublished $481; the three retailers in both months, Ritani, With Clarity and Brilliant Earth, moved +1.3% together, $620 to $628). The 2ct cell fails the second test by the narrowest margin: the blended figure ($1,318, five retailers, against July's six-retailer $1,388, -5.0%) sits 5.04 points from the same five retailers' move (0.0%, $1,318 to $1,318, Blue Nile having dropped out), so it is withheld rather than the rule bent; the per-retailer 2ct figures are shown individually.</li>"
